{"task_id": "Mbpp/116", "solution": "def tuple_to_int(input_tuple: tuple[int, ...]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a tuple of positive integers into a single integer.\n\n    For example:\n    - tuple_to_int((1, 2, 3)) returns 123\n    - tuple_to_int((10, 20, 30)) returns 102030\n    - tuple_to_int(()) returns 0\n\n    Args:\n        input_tuple: A tuple containing only positive integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the concatenation of the tuple's elements.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If the input is not a tuple.\n        ValueError: If any element in the tuple is not an integer or is not positive.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(input_tuple, tuple):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a tuple.\")\n    \n    if not input_tuple:\n        return 0\n\n    str_elements = []\n    for item in input_tuple:\n        if not isinstance(item, int):\n            raise ValueError(f\"All tuple elements must be integers, but found type {type(item).__name__}.\")\n        if item <= 0: # Strictly positive, so 0 and negatives are invalid\n            raise ValueError(f\"All tuple elements must be positive integers, but found {item}.\")\n        str_elements.append(str(item))\n    \n    return int(\"\".join(str_elements))"}
