{"task_id": "Mbpp/308", "solution": "class ProductFinderError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Base exception for errors in the product_finder module.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        super().__init__(message)\n        self.message = message\nclass InvalidInputError(ProductFinderError):\n    \"\"\"Exception raised for invalid input parameters.\"\"\"\n    pass\ndef large_product(list1: list, list2: list, n: int) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the n largest products formed by taking one factor from each of two lists.\n    Returns the products in descending order, handling empty lists or n=0, and validates inputs.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Input validation as per FR1 and ValidationUtil in the design document.\n    if not isinstance(list1, list):\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"list1 must be a list.\")\n    if not isinstance(list2, list):\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"list2 must be a list.\")\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"n must be an integer.\")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"n cannot be negative.\")\n\n    # Return an empty list if either input list is empty or n is 0, as per FR5.\n    if n == 0 or not list1 or not list2:\n        return []\n\n    # Calculate all possible products by taking one element from each list (FR2).\n    products = []\n    for num1 in list1:\n        for num2 in list2:\n            products.append(num1 * num2)\n\n    # Sort the calculated products in descending order (FR3).\n    products.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    # Select the top 'n' elements (FR3). This handles cases where n is\n    # greater than or equal to the total number of products (FR4).\n    return products[:n]"}
