{"task_id": "Mbpp/457", "solution": "class InvalidInputError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input to min_list_finder functions.\"\"\"\n    pass\ndef Find_Min(list_of_lists: list[list]) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the sublist with the minimum length from a given list of lists.\n\n    Args:\n        list_of_lists: A list where each element is expected to be another list.\n\n    Returns:\n        The sublist (of type list) that has the minimum length.\n\n    Raises:\n        InvalidInputError: If the input is not a valid list of lists\n                           (e.g., empty, not a list, contains non-list elements).\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(list_of_lists, list):\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n    if not list_of_lists:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n\n    min_sublist = None\n    min_length = float('inf')\n\n    for sublist in list_of_lists:\n        if not isinstance(sublist, list):\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"All elements in the input list must be lists.\")\n        if len(sublist) < min_length:\n            min_length = len(sublist)\n            min_sublist = sublist\n    \n    if min_sublist is None:\n        # This case should ideally be caught by the empty list check,\n        # but as a safeguard, it ensures a valid sublist was found.\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"No valid sublists found to determine minimum.\")\n\n    return min_sublist"}
