{"task_id": "Mbpp/589", "solution": "import math\ndef perfect_squares(start: int, end: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds all perfect squares within an inclusive range [start, end].\n    It efficiently iterates through potential square roots to identify them.\n    \"\"\"\n    perfect_squares_list = []\n    \n    # Start checking from the square root of max(0, start) to ensure non-negative squares\n    # and to optimize by not checking squares smaller than 'start'.\n    current_sqrt = int(math.ceil(math.sqrt(max(0, start))))\n    \n    while True:\n        square = current_sqrt * current_sqrt\n        if square > end:\n            break\n        # The 'if square >= start' check is already inherently handled by 'current_sqrt' initialization\n        # combined with 'max(0, start)', but keeping it explicit clarifies the range inclusivity.\n        if square >= start: \n            perfect_squares_list.append(square)\n        current_sqrt += 1\n            \n    return perfect_squares_list"}
